INSTALL.txt for ContinueTux

by Sophie Ball
sophieballvaesea@proton.me
https://gamejolt.com/@Vaesea

Version 0.0.7 (in development)

October 04, 2026

REQUIREMENTS
============
  CONTROLS
  --------
    The game can be played with a keyboard. Right now, it's impossible to change the in-game keybinds.

  HaxeFlixel
  ----------
    ContinueTux was programed using the "HaxeFlixel" library for the Haxe
    Programming Language. This means that the game can theoretically run 
    under any environment that HaxeFlixel supports.

    (As of this program's release, this includes: Linux X11 (therefore also
    Wayland), Windows 10+, MacOS, Android (unplayable), iOS (unplayable) and
    HTML5. Flash used to be supported, I'm not sure if it still is)

    HaxeFlixel, HXCPP, Lime, OpenFL, Flixel-Addons and (most importantly) Haxe
    are required to build this project. It sounds like a lot, it really isn't.
    All of these are installed if you follow the normal HaxeFlixel setup.

    You can get started here:
    https://haxeflixel.com/documentation/getting-started/

  Flixel
  ------
    HaxeFlixel (also known as Flixel) is required.

  Flixel-Addons
  -------------
    Flixel-Addons is also required to load Tiled levels.

  Lime
  ----
    Lime is required to compile the game. It's "Assets" class is also used to load
    the levels.txt.

  OpenFL
  ------
    OpenFL is a dependency of HaxeFlixel.

  HXCPP
  -----
    I'm not quite sure what this does, but my best understanding of it is that it translates
    Haxe code to C++ for it to run on Windows, Mac and Linux.

  POLYMOD
  -------
    You don't need to install this yet as it's not even referenced in the Project.xml. However,
    it will be used in the future for mod support.

BUILDING UNDER LINUX
--------------------
  -----------------------------------------------------------------
  Note: Read this entire section before you start issuing commands!
  -----------------------------------------------------------------

  First make sure all libraries listed earlier are installed.

  Then, use the command "lime test linux -debug" to test the game
  in debug mode.

  If you want, you can use anything other than "-debug" such as "-release"
  to build the game in release mode. This causes the HaxeFlixel intro to
  run when the game starts, which tells you that it's a release build.

  You can use "lime build linux" to build the game without automatically
  launching the game.

